course: technology
lesson: 13
title: Heating, water pipes and drains
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Behind the walls and under the floor of every home runs a hidden river system: clean water comes in through one set of pipes, and used water leaves through another.
explain: The clean water arrives from a treatment works, pushed along by pumps and by gravity from reservoirs and water towers up high.
explain: The boiler is the home's heart for warmth: it heats water, and a pump sends that hot water round pipes to the radiators and taps.
explain: When you pull the plug in the bath, the water gurgles away down the drain, under the street, all the way to a sewage works where bacteria help clean it.
explain: Knowing a few of these secrets helps in an emergency: where the stopcock is, what the carbon monoxide alarm is for, and why fat never goes down the sink.
fact: A boiler burns gas, or uses electricity, to heat water, and a pump sends the hot water round pipes to radiators and taps; gas boilers are fitted by registered gas engineers.
fact: A gas boiler can leak carbon monoxide, a poison gas you cannot see or smell, so every home with one needs a carbon monoxide alarm.
fact: Modern condensing boilers catch heat from the steam in their fumes, which is why a white cloud and drips of water come from their pipe outside in winter.
fact: A radiator warms the air next to it, which rises and moves round the room; it heats the room more by moving warm air than by glowing heat; radiators often sit under windows, so the rising warm air meets the cold air coming off the glass.
fact: A wall thermostat measures the room's temperature and tells the boiler to start or stop; turning it right up does not heat the room faster, it only lets it get hotter in the end.
fact: Cold tap water comes from a treatment works, where it is filtered and has a little chlorine added to kill germs, and is pushed through underground pipes by pumps and gravity.
fact: Stored hot water is kept at about 60 degrees to stop germs growing in the tank; run the cold first in a bath and test it with your elbow, because hot water can scald (burn the skin); the hot tap runs cold at first because the cooled water already sitting in the pipe has to come out before the hot arrives.
fact: Under a sink, a U-bend holds a little water that blocks smells from the drain coming back up.
fact: Fat poured down the sink sets hard in sewers and, with wet wipes, makes giant lumps called fatbergs; some have weighed more than a lorry.
fact: When you flush, a valve opens and water from the cistern rushes into the bowl; a float rises as the cistern refills and shuts off the water when it is full; most flushes use several litres.
fact: At a sewage works, waste water is screened, settled in tanks and cleaned by helpful bacteria before the clean water goes back to rivers, and the sludge can be turned into gas for energy.
fact: The stopcock is a tap where the water comes into the house, often under the kitchen sink; turning it off stops all the water if a pipe bursts.
fact: Water expands when it freezes, which can split a pipe; when it thaws, water pours out, so pipes in lofts are wrapped in foam lagging.
fact: Only pee, poo and toilet paper should be flushed, and you keep away from sewers, treatment tanks and any water leaking near electrics.
exercise: What does a boiler do? | Heats water that is pumped to radiators and taps | Cools the water down before it reaches the taps in summer | Cleans the drinking water | Pumps air round the house
exercise: Why does every home with a gas boiler need a carbon monoxide alarm? | A leak of this poison gas cannot be seen or smelled | The boiler beeps too quietly | Carbon monoxide smells strongly of rotten eggs, so you'd notice | The alarm makes the boiler work better
exercise: Why does a white cloud come out of a boiler pipe on the wall in winter? | Steam from the fumes cools into tiny water drops | The boiler is on fire inside and needs putting out | It is grey smoke from coal burning inside the boiler | The pipe is breathing out dust from inside the walls
exercise: How does a radiator mostly heat a room? | It warms the air, which rises and moves round the room | It glows red like a fire | It blows hot air with a fan | It heats the walls first and never the air
exercise: What does a wall thermostat do? | Measures the room's temperature and tells the boiler to start or stop | Measures how much water is in the pipes | Switches the lights on when it is dark | Makes the radiators heat faster
exercise: Where does the water from the cold tap come from? | A treatment works, through underground pipes | A tank that collects rain on the roof of each house | A well dug under each house | The sea, straight through a pipe
exercise: Why is a little chlorine added at the water treatment works? | To kill germs | To make the water taste sweet | To make the water colder | To make the water flow faster
exercise: Why does the hot tap take a while to run hot? | Cold water already in the pipe has to come out first | The boiler is asleep until you call it by turning the tap | Hot water travels slower than cold | The tap must warm itself up first
exercise: Why is stored hot water kept at about 60 degrees? | To stop germs growing in the tank | To make baths quicker and easier to fill | To save as much gas as possible each month | To stop the pipes in the loft from freezing
exercise: What is the U-bend under the sink for? | It holds water that blocks bad smells | It catches lost rings and nothing else | It makes the water run faster | It warms the water before it drains
exercise: Why must fat never be poured down the sink? | It sets hard and blocks sewers | It makes the water too hot | It turns the pipes yellow | It feeds rats in the kitchen
exercise: How does a toilet know when its tank is full? | A float rises and shuts off the water | A clock counts how long it fills | Someone at the water company turns it off | The handle feels the weight
exercise: What helps clean the water at a sewage works? | Helpful bacteria | Lots of soap | Boiling every drop | Strong magnets
exercise: What is the stopcock? | The tap where water enters the house, which turns it all off | The plug in the bottom of the bath | The switch for the boiler | The valve inside the toilet
exercise: Why do pipes sometimes burst when it is freezing? | Water expands as it freezes and splits the pipe | Cold air makes the metal soft | Ice pulls the pipe apart from the outside as it grows round it | The water boils in the cold
exercise: What is lagging on a loft pipe for? | Keeping the pipe from freezing | Making the pipe look neat and tidy | Making the water in it taste better | Stopping mice and rats from chewing it
exercise: Our house has 7 radiators and 2 of them are off. How many are on? | 5 | 9 | 4 | 6
test: dad cranked the thermostat to 30 so it heats faster. does it? | No, it heats at the same speed, just to a hotter end | Yes, twice as fast | Yes, the boiler burns double gas | No, it makes the house colder
test: Ruby reckons radiators sit under windows for no reason. What is a good reason? | Warm air rising there meets the cold air from the window | Radiators like the light | It is the only place pipes fit | So you can dry your socks in the sun that comes through the glass
test: bloody hell, water's pouring through the ceiling! what do we do first? | An adult turns off the stopcock, and keep away from electrics | Turn on every tap in the house | Put your hand over the hole | Switch the lights on to see better
test: What would happen if there were no U-bend under the sink? | Smells from the drain would come up into the kitchen | The sink would never empty, and the water would just sit there | The water would come out hot | The tap would stop working
test: My brother flushes wet wipes because the packet says flushable. Is it a good idea? | No, wipes help block pipes and make fatbergs | Yes, they melt away in the water like toilet paper | Yes, they turn into soft paper as soon as they're wet | No, only because they float back up into the bowl
test: Which is true about the water you flush away? | It goes to a sewage works, is cleaned and goes back to rivers | It goes straight into the sea untreated | It is pumped straight back into your own tap the very next morning | It stays under your garden for ever
test: A new boiler drips water from a pipe outside on cold days. Is it broken? | Not necessarily; condensing boilers drip water from their steam | Yes, it must be switched off at once | Yes, the water is leaking gas | No, it is rain caught in the pipe
test: mate says the cold tap water comes straight from the river, no cleaning. true? | No, it is filtered and treated before it reaches us | Yes, rivers are perfectly clean | Yes, the pipe goes straight to the river with nothing in between | No, it comes from the sea
test: Your little sister is running a bath with only the hot tap. Best advice? | Run the cold first and test with your elbow | Jump in quickly before the hot water cools down | Test it with the tips of your toes, they feel heat best | It's fine, hot water from a tap can never scald you
test: What can a sewage works make from its leftover sludge? | Gas for energy | Drinking water for houses | Paper for schools | Salt for roads
test: Why does a house in a freezing winter need pipes in the loft wrapped? | Lagging stops the water in them freezing and bursting the pipe | It keeps the water cold in summer | It stops the boiler going out | It keeps the pipes from getting dusty
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a U-shaped bend is installed beneath a sink. | It retains water which prevents the ingress of foul odours | It accelerates the drainage of waste water into the sewer below | It heats water prior to its discharge | It filters drinking water before use
exam: It is given that the wall thermostat of a dwelling is raised from 20 to 30 degrees. Ascertain the effect upon the rate at which the dwelling is heated. | The rate is unchanged; only the final temperature is higher | The rate of heating is doubled | The rate of heating is reduced by one half | The dwelling ceases to be heated
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of water freezing within a domestic pipe? | Expansion of the ice may fracture the pipe | Contraction of the ice draws the pipe inwards | The water boils and escapes as vapour | The pipe becomes permanently magnetised
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of cooking fat via the kitchen sink. | It solidifies and contributes to blockages of sewers | It is fully dissolved by tap water and is carried safely away | It is required for the cleaning of drains | It is harmless and recommended practice
